window.PLD=window.PLD||{};PLD["R-tx-2024-06-18"]={"AD-018-24":"Resolution No. AD-018-24\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nCITY OF NIAGARA FALLS-BLUE CARDINAL PROPERTIES\nWAIVER OF TAXES\nWHEREAS, Niagara County, has been in discussions with the City of Niagara Falls to develop a revitalization plan for North Main Street in Niagara Falls, and\nWHEREAS, properties formerly owned by Blue Cardinal Capital (BCC), under the title of Niagara Village LLC, are currently in bank foreclosure and occupy several parcels along the North Main Street corridor, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara Village LLC, has failed to pay property taxes for several years of its ownerships to the county, city and school district creating an outstanding tax liability to the County of Niagara in the amount of $55,887.68, and\nWHEREAS, the Rapids Theatre, also located in the North Main Street corridor, under the title of 1711 Main Street LLC, is currently in a separate bank foreclosure, and\nWHEREAS, 1711 Main Street LLC, has failed to pay property taxes for several years of its ownerships to the county, city and school district creating an outstanding tax liability to the County of Niagara in the amount of $19,538.36, and\nWHEREAS, the City of Niagara Falls, through its Niagara Falls Urban Renewal Agency (URA), is seeking to acquire the BCC properties and the Rapids Theatre through separate agreements with each financial institution and with the assistance of various NYS agencies and the Niagara Orleans Regional Land Improvement Corporation (NORLIC), so as to avoid sole property ownership, which has hampered development in the past, and to control and market the properties, initially to include university student housing and ancillary development thereafter, and\nWHEREAS, the City of Niagara Falls, in its negotiations with the respective financial institutions will, as a part of proposed acquisition by the URA, forego collection of the taxes unpaid to the City, provided the financial institutions enter into an agreement with the URA for the respective acquisitions, if an agreement is not . achieved the priority property taxes would be recovered from any auction sale by the respective financial institutions, and\nWHEREAS, the City of Niagara Falls has requested that Niagara County forego collection of the taxes unpaid up to and including 2024 to the County totaling $75,426.04 for all of the parcels aforesaid, in furtherance of the proposed negotiation and provided the financial institutions enter into an agreement with the URA for the respective acquisitions, if an agreement is not achieved the priority property taxes would be recovered from any auction sale by the respective financial institutions and distributed pursuant to the formula provided by law,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, the County of Niagara agrees and consents to forego collection of unpaid county property taxes up to and including 2024 for properties identified as BCC properties under the title, Niagara Village LLC and the Rapids Theatre under the title 1711 Main Street LLC, upon the specific condition that the respective financial institutions currently holding the respective properties in separate foreclosures, enter into an agreement with the URA to further the development delineated herein, be it further\nRESOLVED, in the event an agreement for acquisition of the respective properties, by the URA, is not achieved with a financial institution, the County of Niagara will obtain its unpaid property taxes on the property, or properties, after a foreclosure auction pursuant to the formula provided by law.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McK.immie.","AD-019-24":"Resolution No. AD-019-24\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nWAIVER OF RESIDENCY REQUEST\nASSISTANT COUNTY ATTORNEY POSITION\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Attorney's Office has permission to fill a vacant Assistant County Attorney position, position number 13 913, this is a critical and important position that is required to satisfy operational requirements of the department and to provide effective legal representation, and\nWHEREAS, due to challenges with the recruitment for qualified candidates despite posting efforts and activities to source candidates for this important position has not resulted in a sufficient applicant pool of Niagara County resident candidates applying for this vacant position, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County Attorney's Office has made diligent and sincere efforts to recruit highly qualified candidates for the position from within Niagara County, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that a one-time Waiver of Residency from the policy to hire only Niagara County residents be\napproved to fill the currently vacant Assistant County Attorney position when the most highly qualified applicant is a non-Niagara County resident or when only qualified candidates reside outside Niagara County.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","CS-031-24":"Resolution No. CS-031-24\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nBUDGET MODIFICATION-ACCEPT NYS HOUSING TRUST FUND AW ARD ENVIRONMENT AL\nDIVISION-DEPARTMENT OF HEAL TH\nWHEREAS, the Environmental Division of the Niagara County Department of Health provides essential services, information and training to protect the health and safety of citizens of Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the NYS Department of Health awarded the Niagara County Department of Health an initial two year (4/1/2024 to 3/31/2026) Housing Trust Fund - Leading in Lead Prevention Pilot Program contract, and\nWHEREAS, the Environmental Division of the Department of Health must use these funds to remediate lead hazards from rental units in state assigned \"communities of concern\", and\nWHEREAS, the Department of Health requests approval to accept NYS Homes & Community Renewal funds,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, the following budget modification be made:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nCM.20.4070.419 43450.04 HTFC  $373,060.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nCM.20.4070.419 74500.01 Contractual  $373,060.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Andres, McKimmie.","CS-032-24":"Resolution No. CS-032-24\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nPUBLIC HEARING FOR\nNCCC FY 2024-2025 MAINTENANCE AND OPERATING BUDGET\nWHEREAS, the County Manager and Budget Director have filed with the Legislative Clerk the tentative budget for the Niagara County Community College for their fiscal year commencing September 1, 2024 and ending August 31, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, pursuant to Section 359 of the County Law, a public hearing must be held, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that a public hearing be held in the Legislative Chambers, Courthouse, Lockport, New York at 5 :45 p.m. on the 6th day of August, 2024 to review the tentative budget, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Clerk of the Legislature is hereby authorized and directed to publish notice of same in the Union Sun & Journal and the Niagara Gazette.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent-Andres, McK.immie.","CS-033-24":"Resolution No. CS-033-24\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nBUDGET MODIFICATION\nRENTAL SUPPLEMENT PROGRAM 2024-2025\nWHEREAS, the New York State Office of Temporary and Disability Assistance (OTDA) did make state-wide funds available for the Rental Supplement Program, and\nWHEREAS, these resources are available to provide rental supplements to individuals and families who are experiencing homelessness or facing an imminent loss of housing, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County has received an additional allocation of $742,819, which is 100% state funded,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County Department of Social Services continue to administer the Rental Supplement Program as approved by OTDA, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Social Services 2024 budget: INCREASE REVENUE:\nA.22.60 I 0.000 3610.01  DSS Admin Revenue  $ 742,819\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.22.6010.000 74000.03  Administrative Costs  $ 742,819\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McK.imrnie.","CS-034-24":"Resolution No. CS-034-24\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY CLERK'S OFFICE-ABOLISH (2) FULL-TIME CLERICAL II POSITIONS./\nCREA TE AND FILL FULL-TIME DOCUMENT CLERK & CASHIER POSITION\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Clerk serves as the Clerk of Supreme and County Courts as well as register of all land transactions and receiver of filings and recordings of numerous and varied documents, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Clerk is committed to providing exceptional customer service to the residents of Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, position changes within the department are necessary to address departmental needs, and\nWHEREAS, the positions we are seeking to abolish were vacated due to retirement and the department has evaluated the responsibilities of the positions which include indexing, verification, issuing and certifying all details of recorded land and filed court documents, issuing certain licenses, accessing highly confidential, non-public records, maintaining confidentiality of those records, and\nWHEREAS, the Document Clerk & Cashier title is more appropriate to these job functions, and\nWHEREAS, the Document Clerk & Cashier title will effectively allow the cross-training of employees between the three sub-departments of the County Clerk's office which include, Actions and Proceedings, Land Records and General Recording, and\nWHEREAS, the Document Clerk & Cashier title has the ability to perform all actions and job duties for all three aforementioned sub-departments of the County Clerk's office allowing for a more cohesive and effective work enviromnent, and\nWHEREAS, the current Clerical II positions have been vacant since January, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, the remaining funds from the vacant Clerical II positions will be sufficient to cover the rate of pay increase resulting in no additional cost to the County, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that (two) Clerical II (vacant as of April 1, 2024), Grade 6, Step I, position numbers 4580 and 134, salary $21.51/hr - $23 .95/hr, be abolished, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that (2) full-time, Document Clerk & Cashier, Grade 6, Step I, salary $23.14/hr - $26.23/hr, positions be created within the Niagara County Clerk's Office, and filled\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Andres, McKimmie.","CS-035-24":"Resolution No. CS-035-24\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY CLERK'S OFFICE-ABOLISH FULL-TIME DOCUMENT CLERK POSITION\n#118/ CREATE AND FILL FULL-TIME DOCUMENT CLERK & CASHIER POSITION\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Clerk serves as the Clerk of Supreme and County Courts as well as register of all land transactions and receiver of filings and recordings of numerous and varied documents, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Clerk is committed to providing exceptional customer service to the residents of Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, position changes within the department are necessary to address departmental needs, and\nWHEREAS, the position we are seeking to abolish was vacated due to retirement and the department has evaluated the responsibilities of the position which include indexing, verification, issuing and certifying all details of recorded land and filed court documents, issuing certain licenses, accessing highly confidential, non-public records, maintaining confidentiality of those records, and\nWHEREAS, the Document Clerk & Cashier title is more appropriate to these job functions, and\nWHEREAS, the Document Clerk & Cashier title will effectively allow the cross-training of employees between the three sub-departments of the County Clerk's office which include, Actions and Proceedings, Land Records and General Recording, and\nWHEREAS, the Document Clerk & Cashier title has the ability to perform all actions and job duties for all three aforementioned sub-departments of the County Clerk's office allowing for a more cohesive and effective work environment, and\nWHEREAS, the current Document Clerk position has been vacant since January, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, the remaining funds from the vacant Document Clerk position will be sufficient to cover the rate of pay increase resulting in no additional cost to the County, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that one Document Clerk (vacant as of October 30, 2023), Grade 5, Step 1, position number 118, salary $22.32/hr - $24.90/hr, be abolished, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that one full-time, Document Clerk & Cashier, Grade 6, Step 1, salary $23.14/hr - $26.23/hr, be created within the Niagara County Clerk's Office, and filled\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","CS-036-24":"Resolution No. CS-036-24\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY CLERK\nBUDGET MODIFICATION -DWYER GRANT\nWHEREAS, the New York State legislature has awarded $185,000 to the Niagara County Veterans Service agency to support a pilot program for veterans suffering from PTSD during fiscal year 2024, and\nWHEREAS, the awarded amount of$185,000 was adopted in the 2024 County Clerk budget, and\nWHEREAS, in May of 2024, the Niagara County Clerk's office was notified that the New York State Legislature has awarded additional Cost of Living Allocation (COLA) funds in the amount of$12,950.00, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Department of Mental Health will advance the Legislative grant funding to the County Clerk's department in the total amount of $12,950.00 upon receipt from the New York State Office of Mental Health, and\nWHEREAS, the 2024 budget will need to be modified to accommodate the increased funding, now, therefore,\nbe it\nRESOLVED, that the 2024 budget be modified as follows:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA. I 0.1410.000.43490.01  Mental Health Program General  $12,950.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.10.1410.000.74400.09  Misc. Expenses Payments Other Agencies  $12,950.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent- Andres, McK.immie.","CS-037-24":"Resolution No. CS-037-24\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY DEPARTMENT OF MENTAL HEALTH & SUBSTANCE ABUSE SVCS-\nCREATE FULL TIME CRISIS SERVICES PHONE AIDE POSITION - BUDGET MODIFICATION\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Department of Mental Health & Substance Abuse Services (NCDMH) is committed to providing exceptional treatment and integrated care services to Niagara County residents in need, and\nWHEREAS, NCDMH has operated the County's Crisis Services 24/7/365 call center since 1971 with trained Crisis Services Phone Aides providing confidential support and assistance to individuals in distress, including those experiencing mental health emergencies or suicidal ideation, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County Crisis Services is a provider for the National Suicide Prevention Lifeline, whose national mental health/suicide crisis phone number, 988 ( similar to 911) was implemented effective July 16, 2022,and\nWHEREAS, both the local Crisis Services line as well as the 988 Crisis and Suicide Prevention Lifeline caller concerns have become of increasing complexity and duration, and\nWHEREAS, call volume has increased due to 988, and\nWHEREAS, the NYS Office of Mental Health is providing additional funding for Crisis Services to offset increased call volume due to 988, and\nWHEREAS, this funding allows for the creation of an additional full time position to respond to the increase in call volume and to ensure adequate shift coverage for this emergency operation related to planned and unplanned benefit time, and\nWHEREAS, NCDMH is committed to and has demonstrated operating in a responsible fiscal manner, and\nWHEREAS, the creation of this position is at no additional current cost to the County, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that one Full Time Crisis Services Phone Aides, Grade 5, Step 1, position number, MHD300100.:XXXXX, salary range $40,779 - $45,492, be created within the Niagara County Department of Mental Health, and filled effective June 19, 2024, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications in the 2024 budget be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.21.4310.000.43490.08  Community Support Services  $36,955\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.21.4310.000.7101 O.OOxxxxx  Position (FT CS Phone Aide)  $ 21,874\nA.21.4310.000.71050.00  Overtime Expense  2,232\nA.21.4310.000.71070.00  Shift Differential Expense  312\nA.21.4310.000.78100.00  Retirement Expense  2,649\nA.21.4310.000.78200.00  FICA  1,868\nA.21.4310.000.78300.00  Worker's Comp  527\nA.21.4310.000.78400.01  Insurance, Health Active Hospital/Medical  6,577\nA.21.4310.000.78400.05  Insurance HRA Employer Contribution  425\nA.21.4310.000.78700.00  Disability  45\nA.21.4310.000.78800.00  Flex 125 Employer Contribution Expense  446\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","CS-038-24":"Resolution No. CS-038-24\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nMH - 2024 BUDGET MODIFICATION - OPIOID SETTLEMENT FUNDS USE\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara is concerned about the welfare of all of its citizens and, as such, formed the Niagara County Opioid Task Force (OASIS) in 2016 in recognition of the broad impact the opioid epidemic was having on our communities, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara entered into and joined a range of litigation against manufacturers, distributors, and sellers of opioids in 2018, and\nWHEREAS, the NYS Attorney General's office and separately States Attorney Generals' offices have negotiated numerous settlements with manufacturers, distributors, and sellers of opioid, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara is in receipt of funds designed to offset the expansive costs and impact of the Opioid crisis on County operations and funds designated for treatment, recovery, and prevention efforts, herein referred to as \"Schedule C - approved uses,\" and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Department of Mental Health &Substance Abuse Services (NCDMH), as the Local Governmental Unit (LOU), is responsible for the planning and oversight of the service system to ensure the availability and continuance of services to individuals with mental illness and substance use disorders who are living in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Department of Mental Health (NCDMH), on behalf of the County of Niagara, has gathered data and input to create a plan for the distribution and use of Schedule C funds through stakeholder forums, public input sessions, and legislative committee meetings, and\nWHEREAS, these planning efforts resulted in the identification of gaps, needs, and key priorities in several areas, including: clinical services; community supports/services; workforce training; diversity, equity, and inclusion; and prevention/outreach. Strategies and solutions which require extensive funding investments have been referred to New York State Office of Addiction Services and Supports, and\nWHEREAS, effective June 19th, NCDMH will partner with the Mental Health Association, who will take lead in the Soul Shop\u2122 Leaders event planning and coordination efforts in partnership with the Niagara County Suicide Prevention Coalition. The Soul Shop\u2122 Leaders is a one-day workshop designed for clergy, staff, lay pastors, and faith-based clinicians to develop soul-safe communities by equipping members to address suicide as a regular aspect of their life and work. This training will address the need for faith-based communities to increase their knowledge and skill sets to make a positive impact on the lives of individuals, and their families, with whom they have contact that have Opioid Use, Substance Use, and co-occurring Mental Health conditions, and now, there be it\nRESOLVED, that the Director of Community Services be authorized to engage in the process to carry out the necessary agreements between the County and Mental Health Association, subject to the approval of the County Attorney's Office, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.21.4310.816.42735.01  Opioid Settlement Funds Restricted  $4,250\nINCREASE EXPENSE:\nA.21.4310.816.74500.01  Contractual Expenses  $4,250\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","CS-039-24":"Resolution No. CS-039-24\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nCOMMUNICABLE DISEASE PREVENTION SOLE SOURCE - NURSING DMSION DEPARTMENT\nOF HEALTH\nWHEREAS, the Nursing Division of the Niagara County Department of Health prevents communicable disease, provides services, information and training to protect the health and safety of the citizens ofNiagara County, and\nWHEREAS, a resupply of Typhoid (TYPHIM) and Yell ow Fever (YF-V AX) is needed in the travel vaccination clinic, and\nWHEREAS, Sanofi Pasteur is the sole distributor for these two vaccines in the United States, and\nWHEREAS, the Department of Health has funding approved in the annual county budget, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Department of Health is hereby authorized to waive Niagara County purchasing policy to purchase Typhoid (TYPHIM) and Yellow Fever (YF-V AX) vaccines without the need to solicit for alternative proposals, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorizes an agreement for purchase not to exceed $19,999.00 to be signed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nApproved for submission.\nMoved by Gooch, seconded by Myers, to implement the portion of Rule 15 that pertains to voting on a resolution as opposed to referring it to committee. Carried.\nMoved by Gooch, seconded by Myers.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent-Andres, McKimmie.","CSS-053-24":"Resolution No. CSS-053-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: June I 8, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION -ACCEPT BYRNE JAG LAW ENFORCEMENT EQUIPMENT GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been notified by the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services that it is the recipient of an Edward Byrne Memorial Justice Assistance Grant Program Law Enforcement Equipment Grant in the amount of $99,960 for the performance period April 1, 2024 through March 31, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, this grant will allow the Sheriffs Office to purchase needed law enforcement equipment including but not limited to respirator masks, Crime Scene camera system, a radar unit, thermal binoculars and winter specialty uniforms, and\nWHEREAS, the 2024 budget needs to be revised in order to purchase the needed equipment, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney review, the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services Edward Byrne Memorial Justice Assistance Grant Program Law Enforcement Equipment Grant may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.17.3645.000 44305.02  Civil Defense Homeland Security  $99,960\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3645.000 72100.21  Law Enforcement Equipment  $77,960\nA.17.3645.000 74300.06  Uniform expense  $22,000\nMoved by Gooch, seconded by Myers.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Andres, McKimmie.","CSS-054-24":"Resolution No. CSS-054-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - ACCEPT FY23 STONEGARDEN GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been notified by the New York State Department of Homeland Security and Emergency Services that it has been awarded $117,125 under the FY2023 Operation Stonegarden Program, whose funding is provided by the U.S. Department of Homeland Security and Emergency Services, and\nWHEREAS, Operation Stonegarden has been awarded to the Niagara County Sheriffs Office for many years for expenses to assist the County in conducting border centric, intelligence driven operations with the goal of reduction or elimination of threat, risk and vulnerability along our nation's borders, and\nWHEREAS, the performance period for the grant is September 1, 2023 through August 31, 2026, and\nWHEREAS, the 2024 budget needs to be amended to accept the revenue, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the FY23 Operation Stonegarden Grant may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.17.3645.000 44305.02  Civil Defense Homeland Security  $117,125\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3645.000 71050.00  Overtime Expense  $17,840\nA.17.3645.000 72100.21  Law Enforcement Equipment  44,200\nA.17.3645.000 74400.09  Payments Other Agencies  50,625\nA.17.3645.000 78100.00  Retirement Expense  2,710\nA.17.3645.000 78200.00  FICA Expense  1,365\nA.17.3645.000 78300.00  Worker's Compensation Expense  385\nMoved by Gooch, seconded by Myers.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Andres, McKimmie.","CSS-055-24":"Resolution No. CSS-055-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - ACCEPT LAW ENFORCEMENT TECHNOLOGY GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been notified by the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services that it is the recipient of a grant under the New York State DCJS Law Enforcement Technology (LETECH) Program 24-25 in the amount of $950,000.00 for the performance period April 1, 2024 through March 31, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, this grant will allow the Sheriffs Office to purchase needed law enforcement equipment including but not limited to law enforcement portable radios, and\nWHEREAS, the 2024 budget needs to be revised in order to purchase the needed equipment, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney review, the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services Law Enforcement Technology (LETECH) Program Grant may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.17.3645.000 43305.02  Civil Defense Homeland Security  $950,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17 .3645 .000 72100 .15  Communications Equipment  $950,000\nMoved by Gooch, seconded by Myers.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McK.immie.","CSS-056-24":"Resolution No. CSS-056-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - STOP DWI FOUNDATION GRANT\nWHEREAS, the New York State STOP-DWI Foundation, Inc. has notified the Niagara County Sheriffs Office of an additional $24,000 in grant funds available to use for High Visibility Engagement Campaigns this year, and\nWHEREAS, the grant funds are utilized for enforcement efforts by multiple law enforcement agencies during peak holidays with the goal of reducing the number of crashes, injuries and deaths on Niagara County roads, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to modify the 2024 budget to utilize these funds, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.17.3315.000 44389.09  Other Public Safety Traffic  $24,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3315.000 74400.09  MisceUaneous Expenses Payments Other Agencies  $24,000\nMoved by Gooch, seconded by Myers.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Andres, McKimmie.","CSS-057-24":"Resolution No. CSS-057-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nHOURS FOR CIVIL DIVISION OF THE NIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has a Civil Division which is mandated by the State of New York with the purpose to serve and execute the numerous legal processes issues by non-criminal courts, and\nWHEREAS, County Law - CNT 206 states that the board of supervisors may fix the hours of the civil office of the sheriff and if the board of supervisors do not fix the hours then the hours are 9 am to 5 pm, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office would like to set the hours of the civil office at 8 am to 4 pm which is consistent with the hours of other divisions in the Sheriffs Office and consistent with the workflow of the office, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the hours of the Civil Office in the Niagara County Sheriffs Office be set to 8 am to 4 pm as permitted under County Law - CNT 206.\nMoved by Gooch, seconded by Myers.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Andres, McKimmie.","CSS-058-24":"Resolution No. CSS-058-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nSOLE SOURCE STANDARDIZATION OF COMPUTER VOICE STRESS ANALYZER\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office utilizes a Computer Voice Stress Analyzer (CVSA) as a tool for criminal investigations and the current CVSA is due to be replaced, and\nWHEREAS, the Computer Voice Stress Analyzer III (CVSA III) incorporates version-unique and specialized processes protected by two US patents (US Patent Number 7,321,855 and US Patent Number 7,571,101), and\nWHEREAS, the NITV Federal Services, LLC  (NFS) is the Sole Source for the Computer Voice Stress\nAnalyzer III as well as the manufacturer approve certification training programs as the exclusive manufacturer/distributor with no US distributors, and\nWHEREAS, NFS is the only Voice Stress Analysis (VSA) manufacturer holding patents for its VSA technologies which can precisely quantify stress in the human voice to evaluate CVSA examination charts as Deceptive or Non-Deceptive with an accuracy level greater than 98%, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that for reasons of efficiency and economy, pursuant to General Municipal Law 103(5), there is a need for standardization for the Niagara County Sheriffs Office in the purchase of Computer Voice Stress Analyzer III, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that it is hereby determined that NITV Federal Services, LLC is the sole source for the Computer Voice Stress Analyzer III, and that the Sheriffs Office is hereby authorized to purchase the CVSA III and Certified Examiner Training directly from NITV Federal Services, LLC without the need to solicit for alternate proposals, such purchase to be made in consultation with the Purchasing Department and in accordance with budgetary procedures.\nMoved by Gooch, seconded by Myers.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McK.immie.","CSS-059-24":"Resolution No. CSS-059-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nSOLE SOURCE STANDARDIZATION OF DRACO SYSTEM\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has a BearCat Armored Vehicle which is utilized by the Emergency Response Team, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has identified a need to purchase a gas delivery system with hard surface breaching head for the armored vehicle to allow the Emergency Response Team to deploy less lethal chemical agents from within the protection of the armored vehicle, and\nWHEREAS, the Sheriffs Office has secured grant funding through the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services to fund the purchase, and\nWHEREAS, the NCSO BearCat is equipped with the DRACO prep package which will only accept the DRACO gas delivery system with hard surface breaching head, and\nWHEREAS, J&N Tactical is the sole manufacturer and distributor of the patented DRACO Gas Delivery System (US Patent 10,696,470) and BH-1 Hard Surface Breaching Tool (US Patent D927,271) as well as the mandatory training for use of the system, and\nWHEREAS, the grant funded purchase price of the DRACO Gas Delivery System with Hard Surface Breaching Head and mandatory training on the system is $25,125, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that for reasons of efficiency and economy, pursuant to General Municipal Law 103(5), there is a need for standardization for the Niagara County Sheriffs Office in the purchase of the DRACO Gas Delivery System, Hard Surface Breaching Tool and Training, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that it is hereby determined that J&N Tactical is the sole source for the DRACO Gas Delivery System, Hard Surface Breaching Tool and Training, and that the Sheriffs Office is hereby authorized to purchase the DRACO Gas Delivery System, Hard Surface Breaching Tool and Training directly from J&N Tactical without the need to solicit for alternate proposals, such purchase to be made in consultation with the Purchasing Department and in accordance with budgetary procedures.\nMoved by Gooch, seconded by Myers.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent-Andres, McKimmie.","CSS-060-24":"Resolution No. CSS-060-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nSOLE SOURCE STANDARDIZATION OF OSCR360 LICENSES AND MAINTENANCE\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has identified a need for an OSCR360 High Resolution Capture Kit for use by the Accident Investigation Unit and the Crime Scene Investigation Unit, and\nWHEREAS, the OSCR360 High Resolution Capture Kit paired system includes software and a 360 degree spherical image capture kit to allow the documentation of scenes including GPS coordinates and compass sensors which can then build and present a case with a true and accurate depiction of the scene, and\nWHEREAS, the OSCR360 High Resolution Capture Kit is available on GSA Contract and the Sheriffs Office also needs to purchase the software licenses and maintenance, and\nWHEREAS, the OSCR360 High Resolution Capture Kit with licenses, maintenance and tripod compatible with the camera are available exclusively from L-Tron Corporation, and\nWHEREAS, the OSCR360 High Resolution Capture kit is available from L-Tron Corporation under GSA Contract for $6,291.54 and the necessary licenses and maintenance with tripod are $14,913.95 but not on GSA Contract, and\nWHEREAS, the purchase of the system, licenses, maintenance and tripod will be made with grant funding,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that for reasons of efficiency and economy, pursuant to General Municipal Law 103(5), there is a need for standardization for the Niagara County Sheriffs Office in the purchase of the licenses, maintenance and tripod for the OSCR360 High Resolution Capture Kit, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that it is hereby determined that L-Tron Corporation is the sole source for the OSCR360 High Resolution Capture Kit, the licenses, maintenance and tripod for the system, and that the Sheriffs Office is hereby authorized to purchase the licenses, maintenance and tripod for the OSCR360 High Resolution Capture Kit directly from L-Tron Corporation without the need to solicit for alternate proposals, such purchase to be made in consultation with the Purchasing Department and in accordance with budgetary procedures.\nMoved by Gooch, seconded by Myers.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","CSS-061-24":"Resolution No. CSS-061-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nWILMAC SOLE/ SINGLE SOURCE DESIGNATION FOR SERVICE\nAND MAINTENANCE OF NICE SYSTEM\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office Communication 911 Center has a NICE Systems Inc. digital recording system which records and plays back the Sheriffs Office telephone system 911 calls and radio system calls, and\nWHEREAS, this system is critical for the function of the Communication Center as well as law enforcement agencies throughout Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office wishes to purchase a maintenance contract to include unlimited break/fix service, software assurance for major version upgrades, software patches, bug fixes and minor version upgrades for $21,395 for 7 /1/24 to 6/30/25, and\nWHEREAS, Wilmac is the only NICE contracted Premier Public Safety Dealer Business Partner in Western New York State, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Sheriffs Office be authorized to waive the requirement for a Request for Proposal or additional bids to purchase maintenance contracts for the NICE Systems Inc digital recording system and that Wilmac be recognized as the sole source provider for the Niagara County Sheriff's Office NICE System and its service and maintenance.\nMoved by Gooch, seconded by Myers.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent-Andres, McK.immie.","CSS-062-24":"Resolution No. CSS-062-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\n2024 BUDGET MODIFICATION - FIRE COORDINATOR'S OFFICE\nACCEPT PAYMENTS FOR FIRE INVESTIGATION REPORT FEES\nWHEREAS, the County Fire Investigation team collected fees for reproducing requested fire investigation reports pertaining to multiple investigations, and\nWHEREAS, under Public Officers Law \u00a787(1), an agency is allowed to charge a fee for reproducing requested records, and\nWHEREAS, the Fire Coordinator's Office received payments from various insurance companies requesting fire investigation reports totaling an amount of $150.00 for January through May 2024, and\nWHEREAS, the Fire Coordinator's Office is reallocating the collected fees to the Fire Investigation Team for needed supplies, at no cost to the County, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following 2024 budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.19.3410.000 41289.08  Other General Gov Income Reimbursement  $ 150.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.19.3410.000 74550.32  Programs Special Task Force  $ 150.00\nMoved by Gooch, seconded by Myers.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","CSS-063-24":"Resolution No. CSS-063-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nSOLE SOURCE STANDARDIZATION OF ANNUAL SYSTEM MAINTENANCE FOR LIVESCAN\nSYSTEMS, MULTI-JURISDICTIONAL MGIS AND AFIS SYSTEMS\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office utilizes FingerRoll Livescan Systems in conjunction with the Multi-Jurisdictional MGIS and AFIS Systems interface in order to collect and track fingerprints, and\nWHEREAS, the Livescan Fingerprint Systems are critical in law enforcement by providing a higher quality inkless electronic means of capturing fingerprints in a digitized format and transmit them to .state criminal justice agencies and the FBI in accordance with FBI Automated Fingerprint Identification Standards (IAFIS) image quality standards, and\nWHEREAS, ID Networks is the sole source vendor for the hardware, software and service for the FingerRoll Livescan Systems which are currently being used by the Niagara County Sheriffs Office, and\nWHEREAS, ID Networks is the sole source vendor to provide service for their Multi-Jurisdicitonal MGIS and AFIS System interfaces which are currently in place at the Niagara County Sheriffs Office, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that for reasons of efficiency and economy, pursuant to General Municipal Law 103(5), there is a need for standardization for the Niagara County Sheriffs Office in the purchase of annual service maintenance for the FingerRoll Livescan Systems and annual service maintenance for the Multi-Jurisdictional MGIS and AFIS Systems interfaces, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that it is hereby determined that ID Networks is the sole source vendor for the annual service maintenance for the FingerRoll Livescan Systems and annual service maintenance for the Multi-Jurisdictional MGIS and AFIS Systems, and that the Sheriffs Office is hereby authorized to purchase the annual service maintenance for the FingerRoll Livescan Systems and annual service maintenance for the Multi-Jurisdictional MGIS and AFIS Systems directly from ID Networks without the need to solicit for alternate proposals, such purchase to be made in consultation with the Purchasing Department and in accordance with budgetary procedures.\nMoved by Hill, seconded by Godfrey, to implement the portion of Rule 15 that pertains to voting on a resolution as opposed to referring it to committee. Carried.\nMoved by Hill, seconded by Myers.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Andres, McKimmie.","CW-010-24":"Resolution No. CW-010-24\nFrom: Committee of the Whole.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nRESOLUTION TO AUTHORIZE THE HIRING OF OUTSIDE COUNSEL\nTO CHALLENGE INTERFERENCE IN LOCAL ELECTIONS IN\nTHE COUNTY OF NIAGARA BY THE STATE OF NEW YORK\nWHEREAS, throughout the history of the County of Niagara regularly scheduled elections for County Officers have been conducted in odd numbered years, and\nWHEREAS, tbe system of holding local elections allows for the electorate to focus on the issues and matters affecting their local governments without competition from campaigns focusing on state and national issues, and\nWHEREAS, the Governor and tbe Legislature enacted Chapter 741 of the New York Laws of 2023, and\nWHEREAS, Chapter 741 of the New York Laws of 2023 amends the Town Law, the County Law, the Village Law, and the Municipal Home Rule Law to move certain (not all) local elections to even numbered years, and\nWHEREAS, Chapter 7 41 was signed into law by tbe Governor on December 22, 2023, and\nWHEREAS, these laws are NOT general laws affecting all municipalities, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara did not make a home rule request for this law which radically alters its elections schedule and process, and\nWHEREAS, tbe Constitution of the State of New York expressly provides that, other than Constitutional Officers, local governmental officers \"shall be elected by the people of the local government, or of some division thereof, or appointed by such officers of the local government as may be provided by law\" Constitution, Article IX. In addition Article IX delegates to the counties of this state tbe power to adopt, amend or repeal alternative forms of government, and\nWHEREAS, it is implicit in the rights delegated to the County of Niagara and the otber Counties of this State by the Constitution to determine the terms of office of the County's Officers and when these local officials are to be elected, and\nWHEREAS, Chapter 741 of the New York Laws of2023 necessarily alters the dates of election and the terms of office of County Officers, and\nWHEREAS, Chapter 741 of the New York Laws of 2023 is NOT a general law, but is a special law affecting local government, and such law was not passed pursuant to a request of two thirds of the County Legislature, as required by law, and\nWHEREAS, Chapter 741 of the New York Laws of 2023 is NOT a general law, but is a special law affecting local government, and such law was not passed pursuant to the issuance of a certificate of necessity by the Governor, with the concurrence of two thirds of each house of the Legislature, as required by Article IX, \u00a72 of the State Constitution, and\nWHEREAS, the claim tbat moving local elections to even numbered years will cause a savings to tbe counties because odd numbered year elections would become unnecessary is patently false, and\nWHEREAS, the combination of offices elected in odd and even numbered years will create an unduly long and burdensome ballot, delays in the polling places, increased costs and frustration for voters and election officials, creating new burdens upon tbe right to vote, which will undoubtedly improperly curtail the voters' Constitutional rights to Free Speech and Free Association (under the State and Federal Constitutions), which are inextricably interwoven with their Constitutional Right to Vote, and\nWHEREAS, the provisions of Chapter 741 of the New York Laws of 2023 are not evenly and equitably applied to all municipal entities in the State, and, further, do not cover the election of constitutional officers in the several counties, and, further deny the citizens of this County the equal protection of laws guaranteed by the New York State Constitution, Article I, \u00a7 11, and\nWHEREAS, other municipalities throughout this state are acting to protect the rights of their citizens from this unconstitutional, ill-conceived, and pernicious enactment by the State Government in Albany, to achieve political goals, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that this Legislature herby directs and authorizes the County Attorney to bring an action in the appropriate Court( s) to have Chapter 7 41 of the New York Laws of 2023 declared unconstitutional, invalid, null and void, and to have the application of said Chapter enjoined, and it is further\nRESOLVED, that the retention of outside counsel to pursue Court proceedings is hereby authorized, and the County shall enter into a contract for counsel with the Law Firm of Perillo Hill, LLP, 285 West Main Street, Sayville, New York, 11782.\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Hill.\nAdopted. 10 Ayes, 3 Noes - Elder, Robins, Speranza, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","CW-011-24":"Resolution No. CW-011-24\nFrom: Committee of the Whole.\nDated: June 18, 2024\nCLAIM SETTLEMENT\nMARY J. KNIGHT V. COUNTY OF NIAGARA.\nNIAGARA COUNTY INDEX NO. El 76416/2021\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara self-insures its general liability coverage, and\nWHEREAS, such loss fund is fully funded and separate from the General fund, and\nWHEREAS, the County Legislature is authorized under Section 6-N of the General Municipal Law to approve claim settlements in excess of $25,000.00, and\nWHEREAS, on December 2, 2020 Mary J. Knight was injured in a slip and fall accident while at TROTT ACCESS Center, located at 1001 11th Street Niagara Falls, New York, which is owned and maintained by Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the case Mary J. Knight v. County of Niagara now presents an opportunity for settlement thereby avoiding additional litigation, trial and adverse judicial determinations, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the authority to disburse the Mary J. Knight v. County of Niagara., litigation, in settlement, is given to the Risk & Insurance Manager in the amount of $40,000.00 upon completion and receipt of a General Release for Niagara County and all other agents and employees of Niagara County and Stipulation of Discontinuance from plaintiffs and County's counsel.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","ED-009-24":"Resolution No. ED-009-24\nFrom: Economic Development and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - REAPPROPRIATE GRANT FUNDS\nBROWNFIELDS ASSESSMENT\nWHEREAS, Resolution ED-019-21 authorized acceptance of a grant in the amount of Three Hundred Thousand Dollars ($300,000) from the United States Environmental Protection Agency for a Brownfield Assessment Project that is 100% reimbursable, and\nWHEREAS, One Thousand Eighteen Dollars ($1,018) was budgeted for the 2024 Budget, and\nWHEREAS, fringe benefits from 2023 were recalculated in April 2024 resulting in an increase of One Hundred Sixteen Dollars and Thirty Cents ($116.30) credited back to the grant account, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nCM.28.6989.611.44989.04  Other Home and Community Services  $116.30\nEPA Brownfield Revenue\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nCM.28.6989.611.71050.00  Overtime Expense  $ 75.03\nCM.28.6989.611.78200.00  FICA Expense  $ 3.87\nCM.28.6989.611.78300.00  Worker's Compensation  $ 1.38\nCM.28.6989.611 74300.03  Mileage  $ 36.02\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent- Andres, McKimmie.","ED-010-24":"Resolution No. ED-010-24\nFrom: Economic Development and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION TO ACCEPT UNUSED AMERICAN RESCUE FUNDS FROM THE NIAGARA\nORLEANS REGIONAL LAND IMPROVEMENT CORPORATION\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Orleans Land Improvement Corporation (NORLIC) is a non-for-profit corporation whose members include the County of Niagara, City of Lockport, City of Niagara Falls, City of North Tonawanda and the County of Orleans, and\nWHEREAS, it's the mission ofNORLIC to cultivate the reuse and redevelopment of vacant and tax delinquent residential and commercial properties through the acquisition, listing and sale to responsible parties, whom provide plans for the rehabilitation and future use, and\nWHEREAS, it is an objective of the land bank to facilitate additional private capital investment in neighborhoods to promote positive housing development, as well as improved quality of life, blight and nuisance removal and increased property values, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara values the mission ofNORLIC and supports its efforts through the transfer of properties and funding, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature unanimously voted to pass resolution #ED-026-21, on September 21, 2021, transferring $1,000,000 of American Rescue Funds to NORLIC, and\nWHEREAS, NORLIC was the recipient of $1,000,000 of American Rescue Funds, and as a pass through recipient was obligated to comply with all the requirements of federal funds under CFDA Number 21.027 and comply with all sub-recipient monitoring compliance as defined in Title 2 Code of Federal Regulations Section 200.332, and\nWHEREAS, NORLIC obligated $250,000 for activities that were compliant with the American Rescue funds and has complied with its reporting obligations, and\nWHEREAS, NORLIC has not committed $750,000, and will not do so by December 31, 2024, and NORLIC's Board of Directors voted on May 31, 2024 to return the unused funding to the County of Niagara, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the County ofNiagara agrees to accept $750,000 from NORLIC.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","ED-011-24":"Resolution No. ED-011-24\nFrom: Economic Development and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nAUTHORIZATION TO CONVEY CERTAIN TAX LIENS TO\nNIAGARA ORLEANS REGIONAL LAND IMPROVEMENT CORPORATION\nWHEREAS, pursuant to Section 902 of the Real Property Tax Law of the State of New York the Niagara County Treasurer, acting in his capacity as Tax Enforcing Officer, acquired tax liens on the following properties commonly !mown as (the Properties)\n130.15-3-2.1  4225 Hyde Park Blvd., Niagara Falls, New York in the Town of Niagara\n130.15-5-10  4401 Hyde Park Blvd., Niagara Falls, New York in the Town of Niagara\n130.15-5-11  4425 Hyde Park Blvd., Niagara Falls, New York in the Town of Niagara\n130.15-5-14  Delaware Ave., Niagara Falls, New York in the Town of Niagara\n130.15-5-15  Delaware Ave., Niagara Falls, New York in the Town of Niagara\n130.15-5-19  4311 Hyde Park Blvd., Niagara Falls, New York in the Town of Niagara\n130.15-5-20  Hyde Park Blvd., Niagara Falls, New York in the Town of Niagara\n130.15-5-21  James Ave., Niagara Falls, New York in the Town ofNiagara\n130.15-5-8  Pennsylvania Ave., Niagara Falls, New York in the Town of Niagara\n130.15-5-9  Pennsylvania Ave., Niagara Falls, New York in the Town of Niagara\n131.09-2-28.2  4287 Witmer Rd., Niagara Falls, New York in the Town of Niagara\n, and\nWHEREAS, the Properties are tax delinquent commercial parcels that require environmental remediation, and\nWHEREAS, Section 1616 of the Not-for-Profit Law of the State of New York authorizes a municipality to enter into a contract to sell some or all of the delinquent tax liens held by it to a land bank, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Treasurer has requested legislative approval to transfer the above tax liens to the Niagara Orleans Regional Land Improvement Corporation (\"NORLIC\") with the understanding that the Properties will be put back on the tax rolls into a productive use, and\nWHEREAS, the benefits accruing the County of Niagara (avoidance of the costs of environmental remediation, demolition and other expenses) are in excess of the potential auction value of the properties, and\nWHEREAS, NORLIC received the aforementioned liens through resolution ED-024-22, and have marketed the tax liens, and\nWHEREAS, NORLIC has been working with developers for the properties mentioned herein, and\nWHEREAS, NORLIC has identified developers for these sites, and will transfer the tax liens to developers to purpose of private foreclosure action, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, pursuant to County Law Section 215 and Real Property Tax Law Section 902, Niagara County is hereby authorized to enter into a contract with NORLIC to sell the tax liens on 130.15-3-2.1; 130.15-5-10, 130.15-5-11; 130.15-5-14; 130.15-5-15; 130.15-5-19; 130.15-5-20; 130.15-5-21; 130.15-5-8; 130.15-5-9, and 131.09-2-28.2 for one and no more dollars, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, a contract, from the prior tax lien through 2024 and all other reasonable and necessary title transfer documents shall be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","ED-012-24":"Resolution No. ED-012-24\nFrom: Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nBROWNFIELD OPPORTUNITY AREA GRANT APPLICATION AUTHORIZATION\nWHEREAS, New York State has funds available through the Brownfield Opportunity Area Program (BOA) to complete county-wide pre-planning to identify and provide preliminary analysis of potential brownfields, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County, herein called the \"Applicant\", after thorough consideration, has hereby determined that certain work, as described in its application and attachments, herein called the \"Project\", is desirable; and\nWHEREAS, \u00a7970-r of the General Municipal Law authorizes State assistance to eligible parties for BOA Program grants by means of a State Assistance Contract (the contract); and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County deems it to be in the public interest and benefit to enter into a contract therewith,\nnow, therefore, be it,\nRESOLVED, that Legislatnre Chairwoman Rebecca Wydysh is the representative authorized to act on behalf of Niagara County in all matters related to State assistance under \u00a7970-r of the General Municipal Law for the Project. The representative is also authorized to: sign and submit the application; execute the contract; request payment advances and reimbnrsements; redistribute contract reimbnrsements as appropriate; submit Project documentation; and otherwise act for all matters related to the Project and State assistance; and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Applicant intends to complete a County-wide Pre-Planning: Brownfield Identification and Preliminary Analysis within Niagara County to identify concentrations of known or suspected brownfields and underutilized properties for future specific planning efforts, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Applicant requests funds in the amount of $225,000, representing no more than 90% of the total Project cost and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Applicant agrees that it will fund the Applicant's 10% share of the Project through in-kind services and/or funding from a federal brownfields grant; and be it further\nRESOLVED, that this Authorization take effect immediately.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","ED-013-24":"Resolution No. ED-013-24\nFrom: Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nAPPROVAL OF ASSIGNMENT AND ASSUMPTION AGREEMENT\nWHEREAS, Globe Niagara, LLC is the owner of certain land in the County of Niagara, and\nWHEREAS, Globe Niagara, LLC and the County of Niagara are parties to an Assignment Agreement dated, September, 2021 regarding a transportation grant concerning certain grants received by the County that were used to improve certain rail facilities on the land owned by Globe Metallurgical, Inc., and\nWHEREAS the Transportation Agreement has a 30-year term and provides for certain continuing obligations of Globe Niagara, LLC as the owner of the land to, among other things, maintain a $300,000 letter of credit or cash security deposit in Niagara County's favor for the term of the Transportation Agreement, and\nWHEREAS, as of August 16, 2021, a cash deposit of $300,000 was made with the County of Niagara to fulfil the security obligation, and\nWHEREAS, Globe Niagara, LLC and Highland Ave NY 3801, LLC, have entered into a Purchase and Sale Agreement which Globe Niagara, LLC has agreed to sell the land to Highland Ave NY 3801, LLC and Highland Ave NY 3801, LLC has agreed to purchase the land from Globe Niagara, LLC, and\nWHEREAS, in furtherance of the Purchase and Sale Agreement, Globe Niagara, LLC wishes to assign, and Highland Ave NY 3 80 I, LLC wishes to assume, the Transportation Agreement on the terms set forth in this Assignment, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature approves of the Assignment and Assumption Agreement between Globe Niagara, LLC and Highland Ave NY 3801, LLC, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to sign said Agreement on behalf of Niagara County; subject to receipt of the approval of the Commissioner of the New York State Department of Transportation.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Andres, McKimmie.","IF-111-24":"Resolution No. IF-111-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nAW ARD OF CONTRACT - DATA CABLING PROJECT\nWHEREAS, the Information Technology Department has prepared specifications and the Niagara County Purchasing Department has advertised for bids for the Data Cabling Project for various Niagara County locations, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account number H597.15.1650.000 72100.26, Information Technology Systems - Capital, and\nWHEREAS, the bids were publicly opened and read by our Purchasing Department on June 4, 2024 and\nWHEREAS, the Purchasing Department and Information Technology examined the bid proposals and the resulting lowest bid is:\nWorking Knowledge  $193,442.00\n435 Lawrence Bell Dr., Suite 3\nBuffalo, NY 14221\nWHEREAS, Purchasing and Information Technology examined the bid proposals, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, and\nRESOLVED, that the contract for the Data Cabling Project be awarded to the lowest responsible bidder, Working Knowledge, and not to exceed $193,442.00 and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature or County Manager is hereby authorized and directed to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IF-112-24":"Resolution No. IF-112-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nAW ARD OF CONTRACT\nLEASING NIAGARA COUNTY REFUSE DISPOSAL DISTRICT LOCKPORT LANDFILLS FOR\nCOMMUNITY SOLAR DEVELOPMENT\nWHEREAS, the Refuse Disposal District in coordination with Economic Development, has prepared specifications and the Purchasing Department advertised for a Request for Proposal for leasing Niagara County Refuse Disposal District Lockport Landfills for Community Solar Development, and\nWHEREAS, the Refuse Disposal District Administrative Board finds that a Community Solar Development project on three (3) closed landfill sites in the Town and City of Lockport financially benefits Refuse Disposal District, County and NYSEG subscribers, and\nWHEREAS, the Refuse Disposal District intent is to enter into a lease agreement with a developer that will install, own, operate, and maintain a solar photovoltaic energy system, and\nWHEREAS, eight proposals were received by our Purchasing Department on May 23, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, a review team consisting of the Niagara County Environmental Coordinator and Environmental Analyst, ranked each response, and\nWHEREAS, the review team recommended an award to BQ Energy Development LLC, 400 Market Industrial Park, STE 32, Wappingers Falls, NY 12590 to develop, build, commission, own, operate and maintain a 2.74 MW ac solar energy project on the closed landfill sites to supply approximately 4,132 MWh or electricity to Niagara County and the surrounding community, and\nWHEREAS, BQ Energy Development LLC, proposed to develop and lease up to 15 acres for the construction and operation of a solar energy system for 35 years with two mutually agreed upon 50year extensions at $1,375/acre with and annual lease escalator of2.0%, and\nWHEREAS, BQ Energy Development, LLC will pay the County a Site Lea Option fee of $10,00 per year for exclusive rights to develop the solar projects for a period of 3 years, with two I year extensions, and\nWHEREAS, BQ Energy Development LLC is a firm solely focused on transforming unusable land, such as brownfields and landfills, into operating solar projects that act as a benefit to the community, and WHEARAS, BQ Energy Development LLC will install a perimeter fence around the entire utilized portion of the site and maintain all vegetation for the duration of the lease, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the award of contract for RFP 2024-34: Solar Energy Development for Niagara County Refuse Disposal District Landfills go to BQ Energy Development LLC, 400 Market Industrial Park, STE 32, Wappingers Falls, NY 12590, for development of a Community Distributed Generation (CDG) project at the Niagara County Refuse Disposal District Lockport Landfill site per the review and recommendation of the RFP review team, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Mc.Kimmie.","IF-113-24":"Resolution No. IF-113-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nTOWNLINE ROAD FROM QUAKER ROAD TO COUNTYLINE ROAD PAVEMENT\nRECONSTRUCTION - TOWNS OF HARTLAND AND SOMERSET\nLOCAL PROJECT AGREEMENT\nWHEREAS, the Townline Road from Quaker Road to Countyline Road Pavement Reconstruction Project, towns of Hartland and Somerset, Niagara County, PIN 5764.29 (the \"Project\"), is eligible for funding under Title 23 US Code, as amended, that calls for the apportionment of the costs of such program to be borne at the ratio of 80% Federal Funds and 20% Non-Federal Funds, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara desires to advance the Project by making a commitment of I 00% of the Non-Federal share of the costs of the Design Phases I-VI and Right-of-Way Incidentals Phases of the project, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the County of Niagara hereby approves the above-subject project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the County of Niagara hereby authorizes the County of Niagara to pay in the first instance 100% of the federal and non-federal shares of the cost of the Construction/Construction Inspection phases of the Project or portions thereof, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to reflect the funding levels set forth by Schedule A in the agreement: INCREASE ANTICIPATED REVENUE:\nH779.15.5112.000 44597.01  Federal Aid Cap Const Hwy Rev  $568,000\nH779.15.5112.000 43591.00  State Aid Cap Const Hwy Rev  $106,500\nINCREASE ANTICIPATED APPROPRIATIONS:\nH779.15.5112.000 72600.01  Infrastructure Roads  $674,500\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that in the event the amount required to pay the full Federal and Non-Federal shares of the cost of the project's Design Phases I-VI and Right-of-Way Incidentals Phases exceeds the amount appropriated above, the County of Niagara shall convene its Legislature as soon as possible to appropriate said excess amount immediately upon the notification by the New York State Department of Transportation, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Chair of the Legislature of the County of Niagara be, and hereby is, authorized to execute all necessary Agreements, certifications, or reimbursement requests for Federal Aid on behalf of the County of Niagara with the New York State Department of Transportation, in connection with the advancement or approval of the Project providing for the administration of the Project and the Municipality's first instance funding of Project costs and permanent funding of the local share of Federal Aid eligible Project costs and all Project costs within appropriations therefor that are not so eligible, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that a certified copy of this Resolution be filed with the New York State Commissioner of Transportation by attaching it to any necessary Agreement in connection with the Project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that this Resolution shall take effect immediately.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent-Andres, McK.immie.","IF-114-24":"Resolution No. IF-114-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nA WARD CONSULTANT CONTRACT - TOWNLINE ROAD FROM QUAKER ROAD TO\nCOUNTYLINE ROAD PAVEMENT RECONSTRUCTION\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works evaluated proposals from pre-qualified consulting engineering firms to assist the County with the Townline Road from Quaker Road to Countyline Road Pavement Reconstruction Project, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account H779.15.5112.000 72600.01, Roads, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the consultant services contract for the Townline Road from Quaker Road to Countyline Road Pavement Reconstruction Project, be awarded to Erdman, Anthony & Associates Inc., 8608 Main Street, Buffalo, NY 14221, for a contract amount not to exceed $799,000, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Contract may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IF-115-24":"Resolution No. IF-115-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nAW ARD OF CONTRACT - PUBLIC SAFETY ROOF AND ROOFTOP UNIT REPLACEMENTS\nGENERAL CONSTRUCTION BID\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works has prepared specifications and the Niagara County Purchasing Department has advertised for bids for the Public Safety Roof and Rooftop Unit Replacements Project, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account number H782. l 5. l 620.000 72200.01, Building Improvements, and\nWHEREAS, the following bids were publicly opened and read by our Purchasing Department on May 23, 2024 as tabulated below:\n1.  Grove Roofing  $593,950.00\n131 Reading Street\nBuffalo, NY 14220\n2.  Weaver Metal and Roofing  $702,000.00\n40 Appenheimer A venue\nBuffalo, NY 14214\n3.  Jameson Roofing Co. Inc.  $892,000.000\n3649 East Lake Road\nDunkirk, NY 14048\n4.  Titan Roofing, Inc.  bid withdrawn\n200 Tapley Street\nSpringfield, MA 01104\nand\nWHEREAS, the Infrastructure & Facilities Committee has examined the bid, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the contract for the Public Safety Roof and Rooftop Unit Replacements Project, be awarded to\nthe lowest responsible bidder, Grove Roofing, 131 Reading Street, Buffalo, NY  14220, in the amount of\n$593,950.00, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Agreement for the Public Safety Roof and Rooftop Unit Replacements Project, may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IF-116-24":"Resolution No. IF-116-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nAW ARD OF CONTRACT - PUBLIC SAFETY ROOF AND ROOFTOP UNIT REPLACEMENTS\nMECHANICAL CONSTRUCTION BID\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works has prepared specifications and the Niagara County Purchasing Department has advertised for bids for the Public Safety Roof and Rooftop Unit Replacements Project, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account number H782.15.1620.000 72200.0 I, Building Improvements, and\nWHEREAS, the following bids were publicly opened and read by our Purchasing Department on May 23, 2024 as tabulated below:\n1.  Parise Mechanical, Inc.  $389,000.00\n1106 Sheridan Drive\nTonawanda, NY 14150\n2.  Greater Niagara Mechanical  $448,600.00\n7311 WardRoad\nNorth Tonawanda, NY 14120\nand\nWHEREAS, the Infrastructure & Facilities Committee has examined the bid, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the mechanical construction contract for the Public Safety Roof and Rooftop Unit Replacements Project be awarded to the lowest responsible bidder, Parise Mechanical, Inc., 1106 Sheridan Drive, Tonawanda, NY 14150, in the amount of$389,000.00, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Agreement for the Public Safety Roof and Rooftop Unit Replacements Project, may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IF-117-24":"Resolution No. IF-117-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nA WARD CONSUL TANT CONTRACT - DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC WORKS\nROAD EVALUATION AND PRIORITIZATION FOR TIP SUBMISSION\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works evaluated proposals from pre-qualified consulting engineering firms to assist the County with the Road Ratings for TIP (Transportation Improvement Program) Project, and\nWHEREAS, the DPW seeks a qualified firm to provide professional services for the evaluation, prioritization, cost estimation, and GBNRTC Form submittal of ten ( 10) street segments, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following line item transfer be effectuated to allow the project to proceed:\nFROM:\nD.15.5120.000 74800.06  Bridges - Repairs and Maintenance  $100,000\nTO:\nD.15.5110.000 74650.07  Roads - Professional Engineering Services  $100,000\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the consultant services contract for the Road Evaluation and Prioritization for TIP Submission Project, be awarded to Urban Engineers of NY, P .C., 403 Main Street, Suite 530, Buffalo, NY 14203, for a contract amount not to exceed $97,857, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Contract may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IF-118-24":"Resolution No. IF-118-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nMANN ROAD BRIDGE OVER MUD CREEK BRIDGE REPLACEMENT\nCONSULT ANT AMENDMENT NO. 1\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-099-23, dated June 20, 2023, awarded the contract for consultant services for the Mann Road Bridge over Mud Creek Bridge Replacement Project to Greemnan-Pedersen Inc., 4950 Genesee Street, Suite 100, Cheektowaga, NY 14225, for a contract amount not to exceed $135,322, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to extend the contract completion date to September 30, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the contract to Greenman-Pedersen Inc., 4950 Genesee Street, Suite 100, Cheektowaga, NY 14225, be extended to September 30, 2024 at no additional cost to the County, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Amendment may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IF-119-24":"Resolution No. IF-119-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nROY AL TON CENTER ROAD BRIDGE OVER MUD CREEK BRIDGE REPLACEMENT\nCONSULTANT AMENDMENT NO. 2\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-100-23, dated June 20, 2023, awarded the contract for consultant services for the Royalton Center Road Bridge over Mud Creek Bridge Replacement Project to Greenman-Pedersen Inc., 4950 Genesee Street, Suite 100, Cheektowaga, NY 14225, for a contract amount not to exceed $135,362, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-016-24, dated January 23, 2024, increased the contract in the amount of$1,000 for the preparation of a Bridge New York application for this bridge, for a revised contract amount of $136,362, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to extend the contract completion date to September 30, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the contract to Greenman-Pedersen Inc., 4950 Genesee Street, Suite 100, Cheektowaga, NY 14225, be extended to September 30, 2024, at no additional cost to the County, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Amendment may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McK.immie.","IF-120-24":"Resolution No. IF-120-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nROY AL TON RA VINE FRONT BUILDOUT\nCHANGE ORDER NO. 1\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-030-24, dated January 23, 2024, awarded the contract for the Royalton Ravine Front Buildout Project to LDC Construction, Inc., 1920 Baseline Road, Grand Island, NY 14072, for a contract amount not to exceed $88,690, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to extend the contract completion date to November 29, 2024 and increase the contract in the amount of $31,700 to provide labor, material, equipment, and supervision for additional work related to hauling, dumping and spreading of materials on site, for a revised contract amount of $120,390, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to allow the project to continue: DECREASE REVENUE:\nH747.15.7110.000 44089.07  Other ARPA State/Local Rec Fund  $50,000\nDECREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nH747. 15.7110.000 72400.00  Land Improvements Expense  $50,000\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nH749.15.7110.000 44089.07  Other ARPA State/Local Rec Fund  $50,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nH749.15.7110.000 72400.00  Land Improvements Expense  $50,000\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 1 to extend the contract completion date to November 29, 2024 and increase the contract by $31,700 to LDC Construction, Inc., 1920 Baseline Road, Grand Island, NY 14072, for the Royalton Ravine Front Buildout Project, for a revised contract amount of $120,390, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Change Order may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IF-121-24":"Resolution No. IF-121-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nREPLACEMENT OF QUAKER ROAD CULVERT OVER MUD CREEK,\nCULVERT NO.15-9\nCONSTRUCTION CONTRACT - CHANGE ORDER NO. 1\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. CW-022-23, dated June 20, 2023, awarded the contract for the Replacement of Quaker Road Culvert over Mud Creek, Culvert No. 15-9, Project to J. Bognar Construction, P.O. Box 4, Sardinia, NY 14134, for a contract amount not to exceed $491,311.97, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to extend the contract completion date to August 7, 2024, at no additional cost to the County, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 1 to extend the contract to August 7, 2024 for J. Bognar Construction, P.O. Box 4, Sardinia, NY 14134, for the Replacement of Quaker Road Culvert over Mud Creek, Culvert No. 15-9, Project, be approved, at no additional cost to the County, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Change Order may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IF-122-24":"Resolutiou No. IF-122-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nPUBLIC SAFETY ROOF AND ROOFTOP UNIT REPLACEMENTS\nGENERAL CONSTRUCTION BID\nCHANGE ORDER NO. 1\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-XXX-24, datedJune 18, 2024, awarded the contract for the Public Safety Roof and Rooftop Unit Replacements Project to Grove Roofing, 131 Reading Street, Buffalo, NY 14220, for a contract amount not to exceed $593,950, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to increase the contract in the amount of $32,682.82 for additional work to remove the existing lightning protection system and furnish and install a new lightning protection system per plans and specifications, for a revised contract amount of $626,632.82, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. I to increase the contract by $32,682.82 for the Public Safety Roof and Rooftop Unit Replacements Project, to Grove Roofing, 131 Reading Street, Buffalo, NY 14220, for a revised contract amount of $626,632.82, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Change Order may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Andres, McK.immie.","IF-123-24":"Resolution No. IF-123-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nOPPENHEIM PARK PAVING AND DRAINAGE\nCHANGE ORDER NO. 2\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. CW-029-23, dated September 19, 2023, awarded the contract for the Oppenheim Park Paving and Drainage Project to Concrete Applied Technologies Corp., d/b/a CATCO, 1266 Townline Road, Alden, NY 14004, for a contract amount not to exceed $781,798.50, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-083-24, dated April 23, 2024, authorized Change Order No. 1 to the contract in the amount of $723,195.00 to provide labor, material, equipment, and supervision to furnish and install additional work as identified on Exhibit A and labeled as Change Order No. I, for a revised contract amount of $1,504,993.50, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to extend the contract completion date to July 31, 2024, at no additional cost to the County, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language, and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 2 to extend the contract to July 31, 2024 for CATCO, 1266 Townline Road, Alden, NY 14004, for the Oppenheim Park Paving and Drainage Project, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Change Order may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IF-124-24":"Resolution No. IF-124-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nAPPROVAL OF CAPITAL PROJECTS - 2024\nWHEREAS, Niagara County Departments have submitted various Capital Project Requests, such projects to be funded by Federal, State, Local and other revenue sources, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature, has established the strengthening of Public Works and County Infrastructure as a priority, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature as part of its efforts to strengthen Public Works and County infrastructure supports: continuing to pursue road and bridge development and maintenance in accordance with limited resources, continuing to pursue building and park structure development and maintenance in accordance with limited resources, the purchase or replacement of sheriff/jail equipment and highway heavy equipment,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Capital Projects, per the list, are hereby reviewed and approved in the 2024 Capital Program.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McK.immie.","IF-125-24":"Resolution No. IF-125-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\n2024 CAPITAL IMPROVEMENT PROJECTS\nFUNDING FROM FUND BALANCE AND FEDERAL/STATE AID\nWHEREAS, the County Manager and Budget Director recommend that the following capital improvement projects be funded using funds from Assigned Fund Balance in the General Fund, General Fund appropriations, and Power Allocation Credits: NCCC Electrical Infrastructure Investments, NCCC Science Lab Renovations, Jail Kitchen Equipment Replacement, Retrofit of Jail Sliding Doors, Communication Console Refurbishment, Communications Console Computer Replacement, Forensic Lab Equipment Replacement, Trott ACCESS Center Improvements, Park HV AC Improvements, Wilson Burt Road Bridge over Eighteen Mile Creek Vertical Down project, Braley Road Bridge over Twelve Mile Creek, Culvert Lining Project, Ewings Road Bridge over Eighteen Mile Creek and Quaker Road Bridge over Golden Hill, Bridge Deck Overlays, Hartland Road Bridge over Golden Hill, Hess Road Culvert Replacement, Hunt Street Road Bridge over Bergholz Creek, Lockport Road Bridge over CSX Railroad, Highway Heavy Equipment, Public Works Facility Design and Construction, Bridge Rail and Guide Rail Safety Improvements, East Canal Road Rehabilitation, Raymond Road Rehabilitation and Upper Mountain Road Rehabilitation, now, therefore be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA 40599.00  Appropriated Fund Balance  $2,823,000\nA 40599.01  Conunitted Fund Balance  $  75,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.07.9901.000.79010.10  Transfer to Capital Reserve  $2,823,000\nA.07.9950.000 79010.00  Transfer to Capital Construction  $  75,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nAH40511.00  Appropriated Capital Reserve  $2,823,000\nINCREASE CAPITAL CONSTRUCTION:\nAH.07.9950.000.79010.00  Transfer to Capital Construction  $2,823,000\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nH790.15.2497.000 45031.10 Interfund Transfers - From Capital Reserve - NCCC  $ 500,000\nH802.l 7.3150.000 45031.10 Interfund Transfers - From Capital Reserve  $ 130,000\nH803.l 7.3150.000 45031.10 Interfund Transfers-From Capital Reserve  $ 380,000\nH804.17.3197.000 45031.10 Interfund Transfers -From Capital Reserve  $ 325,000\nH805.17.3197.000 45031.10 Interfund Transfers- From Capital Reserve  $ 400,000\nH698.15.5197.000 45031.10 Interfund Transfers- From Capital Reserve  $ 413,000\nH719 .15 .1620.000 45031. l O Interfund Transfers - From Capital Reserve  $ 275,000\nH767.15.5197.000 45031.10 Interfund Transfers - From Capital Reserve  $ 400,000\nH73 l.15.5112.000 43501.00 Consolidated Highway Aid Revenue  $ 900,000\nH806.15.5112.000 43501.00 Consolidated Highway Aid Revenue  $ 323,000\nH807.15.7110.000 45031.02 Interfund Transfers-From NYPA Conunitted Funds  $ 75,000\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE TOTALS:  $4,121,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nH790.15.2497.000 72200.02 NCCC Buildings  $500,000\nH802.17.3150.000 72100.07 Jail Kitchen Equipment  $130,000\nH803.17.3150.000 72200.01 Building Improvements  $380,000\nH804.17.3197.000 72100.26 Technology Systems  $325,000\nH805.17.3197.000 72100.04 Forensic Equipment  $400,000\nH698.15.5197.000 72600.02 Infrastructures Bridges - Wilson Burt Rd over 18 Mile Crk  $413,000\nH719.15.1620.000 72200.01 Building Improvements  $275,000\nH767.15.5 l 97.000 72600.02 Infrastructures Bridges - Braley Rd  $400,000\nH731.15.5112.000 72600.01 Infrastructures Roads  $900,000\nH806.15.5112.000 72100.10 Highway Heavy Equipment  $323,000\nH807.15.7110.000 72200.01 Building Improvements  $ 75,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS TOTAL:  $4,121,000\nand, be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Treasurer is authorized to complete the payment to NCCC in the amount of $500,000.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent-Andres, McKimmie.","IF-126-24":"Resolution No. IF-126-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nNIAGARA COUNTY SECTION 5311 CONSOLIDATED GRANT APPLICATION\nWHEREAS, the county of Niagara is submitting a request for a consolidated grant of funds to the New York State Department of Transportation, pursuant to Section 5311, Title 49 United States Code, for federal financial operating assistance not to exceed the amount of$918,867, for the contract period January 1, 2023 through March 31, 2029, for a project to provide a route deviation public transportation service for the non-urbanized areas of Niagara County including the towns of Cambria, Hartland, Lewiston, Lockport, Newfane, Niagara, Pendleton, Porter, Royalton, Somerset, Wheatfield, Wilson, and the cities of Lockport, Niagara Falls and North Tonawanda, to be serviced by Niagara Falls Coach Lines, Inc. during the period January 1, 2024 through December 31, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, the county of Niagara and the State of New York have entered into a continuing agreement which authorizes the undertaking of the Project and reimbursement of the federal and applicable state Shares, and\nWHEREAS, the county of Niagara, is contracting with a third party subcontractor, Niagara Falls Coach Lines, Inc., for the project described above, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of any contracts or agreements, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Chairman of the Niagara County Legislature is authorized to act on behalf of the County of Niagara to sign the application and progress and complete the above named project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, the Chairman of the Niagara County Legislature is authorized to sign any contracts or agreements between the County of Niagara and any third party subcontractor necessary to complete the public transportation project, subject to the approval of the County Attorney.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IL-089-24":"Resolution No. IL-089-24\nFrom: Legislator David E. Godfrey and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE WILSON LIONS CLUB\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Wilson Lions Club has been in existence almost 84 years, and\nWHEREAS, the Wilson Lions Club is starting a \"new\" Leos' program within the club, and\nWHEREAS, the Leos' program will specialize in activities that will help develop life skills with the opportunity to learn important knowledge in the areas of problem solving, decision-making and leadership, now, therefore,\nbe it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Wilson Lions Club by appropriating momes from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nThe Wilson Lions Club  $1,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IL-090-24":"Resolution No. IL-090-24\nFrom: Legislator David E. Godfrey and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE TOWN OF WHEATFIELD LIONS CLUB\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, The Town of Wheatfield Lions Club was chartered in 1973, and is located in the Five Senses Nature Park on Ward Rd, and\nWHEREAS, the Five Senses Nature Park is a 17 acre area, part of which is specifically designed with plantings of herbs and other flowers, as well as a pond for the sight-impaired, and\nWHEREAS, the Town of Wheatfield Lions Club, has the only clubhouse in the area, which hosts a variety of events throughout the year, and\nWHEREAS, the clubhouse is available to rent for family reunions, birthday parties, and boy scouts; and other not for profit organizations can use the facilities free of charge, and\nWHEREAS, the Town of Wheatfield Lions Club Clubhouse is in need of interior upgrades, which will allow for the continued usage of the facilities for public and not for profit activities, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports The Town of Wheatfield Lions Club by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nTown of Wheatfield Lions Club  $2,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent-Andres, McKimmie.","IL-091-24":"Resolution No. IL-091-24\nFrom:  Legislators Anthony J. Nemi, Carla L. Speranza, Richard E. Abbott and Economic Development\nCommittee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE LOCKPORT MIDGET LEAGUE\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Community Partnership funds help support numerous organizations across Niagara County that are important to local communities, and\nWHEREAS, the Lockport Midget League has been providing baseball opportunities for area youth since 1939, and\nWHEREAS, through the Lockport Midget League children not only learn to play baseball, but also the importance of teamwork and sportsmanship, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Lockport Midget League by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nLockport Midget League  $2,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IL-092-24":"Resolution No. IL-092-24\nFrom: Legislator Anthony J. Nemi and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF ORGANIZATIONS IN THE TOWN OF PENDLETON\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Community Partnership funds help support numerous organizations across Niagara County that are important to local communities, and\nWHEREAS, the Pendleton Business & Community Association helps promote local businesses and community pride, and\nWHEREAS, the Pendleton Veterans Association exists to recognize the veterans of our community with gratitude for their distinguished service, and are working on a Home Town Heroes project, and\nWHEREAS, the Pendleton Lions Club serves the community by helping the blind, deaf, physically handicapped and anyone in our community that needs assistance, and\nWHEREAS, the Pendleton Seniors offers important services to our local senior citizen community, and\nWHEREAS, the Pendleton Historical Society plays an important part in preserving and promoting the town's tremendous history for both local residents and visitors, and\nWHEREAS, the Wendelville Volunteer Fire Company is an all-volunteer emergency services provider that protects the Town of Pendleton, and\nWHEREAS, the Pendleton Food Pantry provides food to persons in need residing in the Starpoint School District area, and\nWHEREAS, the Boy Scouts of America (BSA) provides the nation's foremost youth program of character development and values-based leadership training, serving 2.2 million youth members between the ages of 5 and 21, including Boy Scout Troop 98 out of Pendleton Church Center and Boy Scout Troop 47 out of Good Shepard Church, and\nWHEREAS, Boy Scout Troop 47 is designing and building a trail head information kiosk for an Eagle Scout project, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the above mentioned organizations as follows:\nPendleton Community & Business Association  $3,200.00\nPendleton Veterans Association  $1,000.00\nPendleton Lions Club  $1,000.00\nPendleton Seniors  $1,000.00\nPendleton Historical Society  $1,000.00\nWendelville Volunteer Fire Company  $1,000.00\nPendleton Food Pantry  $1,000.00\nBoy Scout Troop 98  $250.00\nBoy Scout Troop 47  $250.00\nBoy Scout Troop 47 Trail Kiosk  $500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McK.immie.","IL-093-24":"Resolution No. IL-093-24\nFrom: Legislator Christopher Voccio and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF NIAGARA HERITAGE OF HOPE AND SERVICE\n(HOLY TRINITY) THROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Heritage of Hope and Service is a non-profit organization that maintains the Holy Trinity historic complex on Falls Street in Niagara Falls, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Heritage of Hope and Service provides much needed services to the community in and beyond the Falls Street corridor, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Heritage of Hope and Service is working to upgrade its facilities, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation ~f funding for the Community Partnership Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Niagara Heritage of Hope and Service by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nNiagara Heritage of Hope and Service  $3,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent-Andres, McK.inunie.","IL-094-24":"Resolution No. IL-094-24\nFrom: Legislator Christopher Voccio and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF NIAGARA STREET NEIGHBORHOOD REVITALIZATION\nORGANIZATION THROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Street Neighborhood Revitalization Organization is a group dedicated to improving the Niagara Street corridor in Niagara Falls, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Street Neighborhood Revitalization Organization is in need of funding for a series of improvements to Gill Creek Park, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Niagara Street Neighborhood Revitalization Organization by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nNiagara Street Neighborhood Revitalization Organization  $6,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IL-095-24":"Resolution No. IL-095-24\nFrom: Legislator David E. Godfrey and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE LEWISTON CHRISTMAS WALK WEEKEND\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the mission of the Lower Niagara River Region Chamber of Commerce, dba Upward Niagara Chamber of Commerce is to strengthen the local economy, represent the interest of business with government, promote community and build business relationships, and\nWHEREAS, the 2024 Lewiston Christmas Walk Weekend has been planned for December 5th through the 8th, 2024,and\nWHEREAS, this over 40-year old tradition highlights the Village of Lewiston merchants, the arrival of Santa Claus to the Village, a weekend of family events and activities, culminating in the Lewiston Christmas Lights Parade, and\nWHEREAS, the event will draw large crowds of visitors to the region from surrounding Western New York communities and Canada, to the benefit of local businesses, and\nWHEREAS, an event of this magnitude can only be successful through full community participation and sponsorships, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Lewiston Christmas Walk Weekend by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nLower Niagara River Chamber of Commerce  $2,500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent- Andres, McKimmie.","IL-096-24":"Resolution No. IL-096-24\nFrom: Legislator David E. Godfrey and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE LEWISTON HARVEST & HOPS FESTIVAL\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the mission of the Lower Niagara River Region Chamber of Commerce, dba Upward Niagara Chamber of Commerce is to strengthen the local economy, represent the interest of business with government, promote community and build business relationships, and\nWHEREAS, the Lewiston Harvest & Hops Festival has been planned for September 28th & 29th, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, this fall street festival will highlight over I 00 artisan vendors, craft beer, and farmers' market, and\nWHEREAS, an event of this magnitude can only be successful through full community participation and sponsorships, and\nWHEREAS, the festival will draw thousands of visitors to the river region from surrounding Western New York communities and Canada, to the benefit of local businesses, and the Niagara County Legislature wishes to support this influx of revenue, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Lewiston Harvest & Hops Festival by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nLower Niagara River Region Chamber of Commerce  $2,500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IL-097-24":"Resolution No. IL-097-24\nFrom: Legislator David E. Godfrey and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF LOCKPORT MAIN STREET INC.\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Lockport Main Street, Inc. is a 501(c) (3) nonprofit dedicated to community and economic growth in downtown Lockport through community programming, special events and tourism & marketing, and\nWHEREAS, Lockport Main Street Inc. organizes and executes special events in the City of Lockport to highlight the small business sector, engage tourists and community members alike and spur economic growth in the downtown sector, and\nWHEREAS, these special events include but are not limited to Chalk Fest and Locktoberfest, where funds are needed for equipment and other expenses to promote these events annually, and\nWHEREAS, this equipment is essential and will help preserve these events in the foreseeable future, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports Lockport Main Street, Inc. by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nLockport Main Street, Inc.  $1,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Andres, McK.immie.","IL-098-24":"Resolution No. IL-098-24\nFrom: Legislators Richard L. Andres, Randy R. Bradt, Jesse P. Gooch and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE NORTH TONAWANDA FOOTBALL HALL OF FAME\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the North Tonawanda Hall of Fame is a not for profit corporation organized for promoting both history and present day North Tonawanda High School Football, and\nWHEREAS, for over 50 years, the North Tonawanda Football Hall of Fame has honored nearly 200 players, coaches and volunteer boosters with induction into the Hall of Fame, and\nWHEREAS, the North Tonawanda Football Hall of Fame is requesting funding to purchase an automatic external defibrillator to be place inside the building, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the North Tonawanda Football Hall of Fame by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nNorth Tonawanda Football Hall of Fame  $2,100.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McK.immie.","IL-099-24":"Resolution No. IL-099-24\nFrom:  Legislators Christopher A. Robins, Christopher J. McK.immie, Jeffrey Elder, Christopher Voccio and\nEconomic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF HANDS HEALING HEROES\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Hands Healing Heroes, creates the Field of Flags each year in the City ofNiagara Falls, and\nWHEREAS, the Hands Healing Heroes is a benefit to the City of Niagara Falls, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature wishes to support the Hands Healing Heroes, in their commitment to our local Veterans, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Hands Healing Heroes, by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nHands Healing Heroes  $ 250\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McK.immie.","IL-100-24":"Resolution No. IL-100-24\nFrom: Legislator David E. Godfrey and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE TOWN OF WHEATFIELD PARK PAVILION\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Town of Wheatfield completed a new inclusive playground at Fairmount Park in 2023, and\nWHEREAS, this playground allows children of all abilities to play in a safe manner, and\nWHEREAS, the Town of Wheatfield is planning to add an observation pavilion next to the playground, so that parents have a shaded area to observe their children at play, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of the Town of Wheatfield by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nTown of Wheatfield  $5,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent- Andres, McK.immie.","IL-101-24":"Resolution No. IL-101-24\nFrom: Legislator David E. Godfrey and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE VILLAGE OF WILSON AND THE\nWILSON LEON FESTIVAL THROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Village of Wilson will be hosting the LEON Festival a celebration of being halfway to Christmas, on June 15, 2024 and\nWHEREAS, the Village of Wilson is requesting funds to help support this event, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of the Village of Wilson by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nVillage of Wilson - LEON Festival  $750.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IL-102-24":"Resolution No. IL-102-24\nFrom: Legislator David E. Godfrey and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE WILSON COMMUNITY LIBRARY\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Wilson Community Library is committed to excellent service and providing access to quality materials as a way to empower the community and connect people and ideas to inspire lifelong learning through curiosity and creativity, and\nWHEREAS, the library is currently undergoing several upgrades, and is seeking assistance to replace the circulation desk at the library, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of the Wilson Community Library by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nWilson Community Library  $5,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IL-103-24":"Resolution No. IL-103-24\nFrom: Legislator David E. Godfrey and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE TOWN OF CAMBRIA CAR SHOW\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Town of Cambria will be hosting a community car show on July 13, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, the Town of Cambria is excited to offer this event to the community, which will include a variety of vendors and food trucks, along with entertainment, and\nWHEREAS, the Town of Cambria is seeking assistance to ensure a safe, enjoyable, and memorable experience for all those in attendance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Town of Cambria by appropriating momes from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nTown of Cambria  $3,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IL-104-24":"Resolution No. IL-104-24\nFrom: Legislator Irene M. Myers and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF SPECIAL OLYMPICS NEW YORK\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, for over 55 years Special Olympics has touched the lives of many people in the WNY community, providing inclusive opportunities for children and adults with intellectual disabilities to discover and unleash the champion within, and\nWHEREAS, individuals who participate learn to overcome defeat, enjoy the thrill of spmis, taste victory and stay active for life, and\nWHEREAS, Special Olympics is planning their first fundraiser in Niagara County, with all proceeds go directly to our local athletes, allowing them to train and compete without being charged, and\nWHEREAS, Special Olympics needs support for this program in order for it. to continue to offer the training and competitions, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorize the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Special Olympics of New York by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership as follows:\nSpecial Olympics of New York  $500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McK.immie.","IL-105-24":"Resolution No. IL-105-24\nFrom: Legislators Richard L. Andres, Randy R. Bradt, Jesse P. Gooch and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF GRATWICK HOSE COMPANY INC.\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Gratwick Hose Company and the North Tonawanda Volunteer Firemen's Association will be hosting the armual Firemen's Inspection Day Parade on June 15, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, this Firemen's Inspection Day Parade is the longest running in New York State, and\nWHEREAS, an event like this can only be successful through full community participation and sponsorship,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Gratwick Hose Company, Inc. by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nGratwick Hose Company, Inc.  $1,500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Mc.Kimmie.","IL-106-24":"Resolution No. IL-106-24\nFrom: Legislator Irene M. Myers and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE RANSOMVILLE VOLUNTEER FIRE\nCOMPANY THROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Ransomville Volunteer Fire Company has been serving the residents of the Town of Porter and Ransom ville Hamlet for 100 years, and\nWHEREAS, the Ransomville Volunteer Fire Company is always raising money for new equipment that offers protection and emergency services to the community as well as providing safety equipment to the first responders, and\nWHEREAS, funds are always needed to keep equipment up to date, and continually train the volunteers, and\nWHEREAS, the volunteers of the Ransomville Fire Company hold programs offered to community participants socially and educationally, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorize the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through Resolution CW-001-23, and it be further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports Ransomville Fire Company by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nRansomville Volunteer Fire Company  $500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Mc.Kimmie.","IL-107-24":"Resolution No. IL-107-24\nFrom: Legislator Shawn A. Foti and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE NEWFANE SOCCER CLUB\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Newfane Soccer Club is a recreational soccer league dedicated to meeting the needs of young people in the Town of Newfane and surrounding areas, and\nWHEREAS, the Newfane Soccer Club is dedicated to providing youth with a recreational program that helps them develop soccer skills, foster team participation and sportsmanship, in a safe environment, and\nWHEREAS, the Newfane Soccer Club is purchasing an Automated External Defibrillator (AED), to protect the players and community members in the event of a medical emergency, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Newfane Soccer Club by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nNewfane Soccer Club  $1,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McK.immie.","IL-108-24":"Resolution No. IL-108-24\nFrom: Legislators Richard L. Andres, Randy R. Bradt, Jesse P. Gooch and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF CARNEGIE ART CENTER\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Carnegie Art Center is a 501 (c)3 that is located in the City of North Tonawanda, and\nWHEREAS, the Carnegie Art Center is always looking for new ways to serve the artists and community members who participate in the various events at the center, and\nWHEREAS, this year the Carnegie Art Center is planning to host the Carnegie Art Spree and Live on the Lawn events, and\nWHEREAS, events like this can only be successful through full community participation and sponsorship, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Carnegie Art Center by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nCarnegie Art Center  $3,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McK.immie.","IL-109-24":"Resolution No. IL-109-24\nFrom: Legislators Richard L. Andres, Anthony J. Nemi, Shawn A. Foti. Randy R. Bradt, Jeffrey Elder and Parks, Recreation, Tourism Ad Hoc Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nSUPPORT FOR TOWN OF HARTLAND ENHANCED RECREATIONAL ACCESS AND\nCONNECTIONS WITH GREENWAY PROJECT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Power Coalition (\"NPC\") and the New York Power Authority agreed to a licensing settlement for the next 50 years for the Niagara Power Project, and\nWHEREAS, that settlement allows Niagara County to receive, through NPC Host Community Standing Committee (\"HCSC\"), up to $390,000 annually to fund projects which are found to be consistent with the Niagara Greenway projects along the Greenway Trail, and\nWHEREAS, the Town of Hartland has made application to Niagara County's Ad Hoc Committee on Parks, Recreation and Tourism for Greenway funds to support its Enhanced Recreational Access and Connections within Greenway Project for improvement of the connection of walkways centrally located, and the enhancements of the Hartland Town Park by proposing the addition of multiuse courts, a pavilion and playground equipment as the park is an integral part of the community, and\nWHEREAS, the Town of Hartland Project will give visitors a recreational area to enjoy the outdoors and the project will improve the trails and walking areas to promote a wider audience to participate in outdoor recreation and encourage the use of the Greenway trail, and\nWHEREAS, the improvements and the wayfinding signage throughout the will help connect Hartland Town Park and the Canal Corridor recreational areas to the development of the region as a whole, and\nWHEREAS, Town of Hartland Enhanced Recreational Access and Connections within Greenway Project meets the guiding principles of the Niagara River Greenway, and\nWHEREAS, the Ad Hoc Committee for Parks, Recreation and Tourism appointed by the Niagara County Legislature to review projects brought to Niagara County seeking funding from Niagara County through the Niagara Power Coalition and the Host Community Standing Committee, has recommended that Niagara County approve and support this project, and\nWHEREAS, Town of Hartland Enhanced Recreational Access and Connections within Greenway has the support and approval of Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, 50% of this Project will be comprised of a matching grant funding through the Town of Hartland, Niagara County and the Griggs Lewis Foundation; the Town is requesting the sum of $200,000 of the total project cost of $340,000, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature does hereby support and sponsor the funding of up to $180,000.00, for the Town of Hartland Enhanced Recreational Access and Connections Within Greenway Project when presented to the Niagara Power Coalition and the Host Community Standing Committee.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimmie.","IL-110-24":"Resolution No. IL-110-24\nFrom: Legislators Richard L. Andres, Anthony J. Nemi, Shawn A. Foti, Randy R. Bradt, Jeffrey Elder and Parks Recreation, Tourism Ad Hoc Committee.\nDated: June 18, 2024.\nSUPPORT FOR THE CAROUSEL SOCIETY OF THE NIAGARA FRONTIER\nMUSIC ROOM PROJECT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Power Coalition (\"NPC\") and the New York Power Authority agreed to a licensing settlement for the next 50 years for the Niagara Power Project, and\nWHEREAS, that settlement allows Niagara County to receive, through NPC Host Community Standing Committee (\"HCSC\"), up to $390,000 annually to fund projects which are found to be consistent with the Niagara Greenway projects along the Greenway Trail, and\nWHEREAS, the Carousel Society of the Niagara Frontier, Inc. operates the Herschell Carrousel Factory Museum and has made application to the Niagara County's Ad Hoc Committee on Recreation and Tourism for additional Greenway funds to complete its Carousel Society of the Niagara Frontier Music Room project, and\nWHEREAS, the Carousel Society of the Niagara Frontier, Inc. wishes to complete the restoration to the Allan Herschell complex, a portion which collapsed in 1995, in order to increase the capacity of the Carrousel Museum to fulfill its mission and serve its visitors, by providing an exhibit space for the band organ collection, interpretation of the North Tonawanda band organ manufacturing heritage, demonstration of the roll making equipment, STEM and Music Arrangement lessons for students and an increased tourism attraction, and\nWHEREAS, in 2017, the Niagara County Legislature supported and sponsored the initial funding of $110,000.00 for the Carousel Society of the Niagara Frontier Music Room project, and\nWHEREAS, certain state funding was held up until recently which resulted in an increase in the price of materials and labor that increased the project cost to $1,082,170, leaving the applicant, after finding additional funding, a shortfall of $170,000, and\nWHEREAS, the Carousel Society of the Niagara Frontier Music Room Project meets the guiding principles of the Niagara Greenway, and\nWHEREAS, the proposed Carousel Society of the Niagara Frontier Music Room project will promote increased access to the County's historical and cultural sites, and\nWHEREAS, the ad hoc committee for recreation and tourism fund appointed by the Niagara County Legislature to review projects brought to Niagara County seeking funding from Niagara County through the Niagara Power Coalition and the Host Community Standing Committee has recommended that Niagara County approve and support this project, and\nWHEREAS, after receipt of grants and other funding for the project, the remaining $170,000 of funds is being requested, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature does hereby support and sponsor the funding of up to $170,000.00 for the Carousel Society of the Niagara Frontier Music Room project when presented to the Niagara Power Coalition and the Host Community Standing Committee.\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature is hereby, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, McKimrnie."};
